            <ArticleTitle>[Extracorpreal shockwave lithotripsy in treatment of bilateral ureteral calculi with renal colic during emergency].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>189-90</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To evaluate the clinical efficacy of extracorporeal shockwave lithotripsy (ESWL) for bilateral ureteral stones with renal colic in emergency.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">We retrospectively analyzed the clinical data of 86 patients suffered with sudden renal colic due to bilateral ureteral stones and treated with ESWL between January 2005 and January 2009.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The success rate was 74.4% after a single ESWL session, and the overall success rate was 82.6%. Significant difference in stone length was observed between successful group and failed group (P&lt;0.01). The stone position did not produce significant impact on the outcome of the treatment (P&gt;0.05).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">ESWL is an effective treatment modality in emergency for small-length and short-term obstruction bilateral ureteral stones with remal colic.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
